        {"sourceId": "p1", "mode": "text", "excerptId": "excerpt-1-1"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "source-2",
      "title": "Definition, terminology and classification",
      "kind": "prose",
      "paragraphs": [
        "An ordinary differential equation involves an unknown function of one independent variable and one or more of its derivatives. The guide uses ODEs to describe quantities that change with time, distance or position. A solution is a function that satisfies the equation on an appropriate interval.",
        "The page introduces order, degree and linearity, then applies these definitions in Example 1 and Example 2. Use the original equations alongside the following sequence."
      ],
      "steps": [
        "Identify the highest derivative appearing in the equation to determine its order.",
        "Determine the power of the highest-order derivative after the equation has been made polynomial in its derivatives to identify its degree.",
        "Check whether the dependent variable and its derivatives occur only to the first power and are not multiplied together. The guide defines an equation meeting these conditions as linear.",
        "In Example 1, the guide identifies the highest derivative as y''' and states that the order is 3 and the degree is 1.",
        "In Example 2, the guide classifies the equation as first-order linear because y and y' occur only to the first power and are not multiplied together."
      ],
      "evidence": [
        {"sourceId": "p2", "mode": "text", "excerptId": "excerpt-2-1"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "source-3",
      "title": "Separation of variables",
      "kind": "example",
      "paragraphs": [
        "A separable differential equation can be rearranged to put terms involving the dependent variable on one side and terms involving the independent variable on the other. The guide then integrates both sides.",
        "Example 3 presents separation without an initial condition. Example 4 adds an initial condition to determine the constant. The steps below follow the existing examples rather than supply a new solution."
      ],
      "steps": [
        "In Example 3, begin with the source equation and separate the variable terms as shown.",
        "Follow the integration line, then the source's resulting expression for y.",
        "In Example 4, begin with the source equation and its initial condition, y(0) = 5.",
        "Follow the separation line and then the integration line, which also gives an expression for y containing C.",
        "The guide applies the initial condition and states that C=5.",
        "Read the final expression for y in the original example with that constant applied."
      ],
      "evidence": [
        {"sourceId": "p3", "mode": "text", "excerptId": "excerpt-3-1"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "source-4",
      "title": "First-order linear equations and integrating factors",
      "kind": "example",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The guide presents a standard form for a first-order linear ODE and explains that it is commonly solved using an integrating factor. Multiplication by the integrating factor converts the left-hand side into the derivative of the integrating factor multiplied by y.",
        "Example 5 shows this method in sequence. Consult the original page for the exact standard form, integrating-factor expression and each mathematical line."
      ],
      "steps": [
        "Begin with the equation given in Example 5.",
        "Identify P(x) as shown and follow the source's construction of the integrating factor.",
        "Multiply the equation through by that integrating factor, as the example shows.",
        "Read the next line, which expresses the left-hand side as a derivative.",
        "Follow the integration line, including the arbitrary constant.",
        "Read the final expression for y given by the source."
      ],
      "evidence": [
        {"sourceId": "p4", "mode": "text", "excerptId": "excerpt-4-1"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "source-5",
      "title": "Initial-value problems and rate of change",
      "kind": "example",
      "paragraphs": [
        "An initial-value problem gives a differential equation together with an initial condition. The guide says that the condition determines the arbitrary constant in the general solution.",
        "An ODE may describe a rate of change. If y represents an amount at time t, the derivative with respect to t represents its instantaneous rate of change. An initial condition specifies the starting state. The general initial-condition notation is obscured in the supplied parsed text, so it is not reconstructed here."
      ],
      "steps": [
        "Read the equation and the condition y(1)=3 in Example 6.",
        "Follow the source's integration line to obtain its expression containing C.",
        "Follow the substitution of the initial condition. The guide states that C=4.",
        "Read the source's final expression with the constant applied."
      ],
      "evidence": [
        {"sourceId": "p5", "mode": "text", "excerptId": "excerpt-5-1"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "source-6",
      "title": "Second-order linear equations and distinct roots",
      "kind": "example",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The guide introduces a common second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. It uses a characteristic equation to determine the form of the solution and lists forms for distinct real roots and a repeated root.",
        "Some root and constant labels are obscured in the supplied parsed text and appear differently in the supplied excerpt. Their intended labels cannot be established here. Refer to the original for exact notation; this adaptation does not repair them."
      ],
      "steps": [
        "Follow the assumed exponential form and its substitution into the equation to obtain the source's characteristic equation.",
        "Read the source's distinction between distinct real roots and a repeated root, while noting the unclear labels.",
        "In Example 7, begin with the displayed second-order equation and follow its characteristic equation.",
        "Follow the source's factorization, which gives r=2 and r=3.",
        "Read the final solution form in the original example, retaining the uncertainty about the constant labels."
      ],
      "evidence": [
        {"sourceId": "p6", "mode": "text", "excerptId": "excerpt-6-1"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "source-7",
      "title": "Repeated roots and source revision checklist",
      "kind": "example",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The guide states that repeated roots require a second independent solution containing x. Example 8 presents this case. The constant labels in its solution and in some listed formulas are obscured in the supplied parsed text; this adaptation does not reconstruct them.",
        "The source revision checklist asks whether you can identify order and degree, recognize a separable equation, find an integrating factor for a first-order linear equation, apply an initial condition to find an arbitrary constant, and form and solve a characteristic equation for a second-order homogeneous ODE. The page also lists key formulas for separation, linear first-order equations, second-order constant coefficients, distinct roots and repeated roots. Consult the original for their exact expressions."
      ],
      "steps": [
        "Begin with the equation displayed in Example 8.",
        "Follow the source's characteristic equation and its factorization.",
        "The example identifies the repeated root as r=2.",
        "Read the source's final solution form, noting the unclear constant labels.",
        "Continue through the source's revision checklist in its listed order.",
        "Use the source's key-formula list alongside the relevant earlier examples, without replacing the unclear notation."
      ],
